1
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:15,580
What was your launch experience like?

2
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:20,670
Our launch experience was phenomenal.

3
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:26,350
I don't think we have the the words in the English language to describe spaceflight and what it feels like

4
00:00:26,470 --> 00:00:29,790
You know, it literally felt like we were being slingshot it off the earth

5
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:33,310
You know, you've got millions of pounds of thrust immediately

6
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:38,910
And we were constantly under G and then I like to say it's kind of like a cartoon

7
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:43,790
Right where you're being pushed back into your seat being accelerated and then when the engine cuts off

8
00:00:43,990 --> 00:00:46,830
It's like dad slammed on the brakes and all the kids are on the windshield

9
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:51,190
And then we went right back into the seats again and built up the G's and you know

10
00:00:51,190 --> 00:00:54,030
In a matter of nine minutes, we were in orbit in the earth orbit

11
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:56,830
So it is a core memory that I will never forget

12
00:00:58,230 --> 00:01:00,590
Yes, since that was my second flight

13
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:05,030
I had more kind of room in my mind to think about my surroundings

14
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:10,070
So I was actually kind of really nervous compared to my first flight

15
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:16,430
Just before the launch come down but as soon as the engine light up everybody just got excited

16
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:18,710
Oh, we're like a whoo, let's go

17
00:01:21,030 --> 00:01:24,790
What are the next items on your space exploration bucket list?

18
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:28,070
Oh, that's a good question

19
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:33,590
So the first thing I would like to do is work as a flight controller again

20
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:37,430
Because now I have a second space flight experience

21
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:44,230
So it's my kind of responsibility to share my experiences with my colleagues on the ground

22
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:46,710
So that's my next step

23
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:55,030
And hopefully I can get on the next the future Artemis mission as well as an activational

24
00:01:57,210 --> 00:02:01,530
Space bucket list this one is uh, it's a great question rent similar to talk

25
00:02:01,530 --> 00:02:07,450
You know, I think I feel a great sense of duty to bring our experience back to the ground and help the people on board

26
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:12,170
Right now, you know, we have people on board the ISS working and living 24 hours a day

27
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:16,010
And so it's on us to bring our experience and help the ground team support them as best as we can

28
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:20,170
Would love to go back to space in any capacity that NASA will let me so

29
00:02:20,810 --> 00:02:23,850
You know, whether that's back to the space station or onto the moon with Artemis

30
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:26,650
Hopefully I can stay healthy enough and

31
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:32,540
And eventually get back out there. What was your favorite science experiment to work on?

32
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:38,380
Favorite science experiment is so tough to decide. Um, we worked on a lot of different science while we were up there

33
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:42,460
Um, you know, I did more the majority of the ring-shear drop experiment

34
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:46,380
So looking at protein crystals and how they shear at the water interface

35
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:47,420
and really

36
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:56,220
Watching that with detailed cameras and changing out those samples and allowing them to build computer models of how that works

37
00:02:56,220 --> 00:02:58,860
So that we can inform pharmaceutical manufacturing

38
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:06,940
And those pharmaceuticals will help, you know, with Parkinson's and cancer and Alzheimer's. So I think, you know, we have a very small portion of the science on station

39
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:08,940
It's, you know, an hour a day, maybe

40
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:15,340
But it's somebody's life work that you're working on. Um, and then it's something that can help further humanity and help those in need

41
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:20,540
Since I measured material science in my university

42
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:24,860
My favorite experiment is a electro-static levitation furnace

43
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:33,660
So instead of building a furnace with metal, we use a electro-static force to levitate our metal samples

44
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:38,140
So that we can get a precise data under the high temperature

45
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:45,340
And we can utilize that kind of data to develop new material on the ground. So that's a cool experience

46
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:51,180
Toc, what was it like returning to the space station and how has it changed since your first mission?

47
00:03:51,340 --> 00:04:01,340
Yeah, it's like going back home. You're going back your second home. So as soon as I got there, it took me much less days

48
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:10,300
to get re-adapted to the microgravity compared to my first space flight. So which was good. We have now so good, like, internet connectivity

49
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:19,340
Oh, while we were in the station. So I think it's a good experience to be able to go back to the space station

50
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:26,860
in the station. So I felt the space station program is getting still, like, bigger and bigger

51
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:36,700
Nicole, how did your career as a Division I volleyball player at the U.S. Air Force Academy help prepare you for a career as an astronaut?

52
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,900
Oh man, you know, I love to talk about, especially with little kids, you know,

53
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:46,780
hard work and teamwork for the two things that will get you wherever you want to go.

54
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:54,220
And so being on a team, like the volleyball team at the Air Force Academy really taught me how to take care of other people, especially in high pressure situations

55
00:04:55,100 --> 00:05:02,220
and how to care about those around you, even when you're struggling or when you need someone on for the day. And so

56
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:09,180
things just generally learning how to live and work on a team is so important and it will get you anywhere you want.

57
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:14,780
But it definitely benefits you when you're, you know, at space and you're on the space station with six other humans for,

58
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:19,980
